# Control plane: the files that decide what every later agent reads, runs or
# is allowed to merge. This file is the map of those paths. Since 2026-11-24
# the owner's review is no longer required for them ("replace my review with
# thorough cysec and reliability and liability checks not my human review");
# the `main` ruleset has "Require review from Code Owners" off. Instead
# required checks run main's rules: `risk-review`
# (platform/security/risk_review.py; docs/security/risk-review.md) treats a
# path below as sensitive, and `adversarial-gate`
# (platform/ci/adversarial_gate/gate.py) puts a PR touching one in its
# control-plane tier: the strictest rules plus an LLM red-team review. Agent
# instructions (CLAUDE.md, prompts/*.md) are not listed here since
# 2026-11-24, but the gate's config keeps them in that tier. Workflows not
# named priv-*.yml stay out of it (the `ci-policy` check keeps those
# read-only and secret-free).
#
# Why each line is here: docs/security/agent-hardening.md. The guard in
# platform/ci/control_plane_guard.py (run by the gate from main against the
# PR's copy of this file) fails if a line it expects goes missing. The last
# matching line wins, so the unowned exemptions sit at the bottom.

# Code that agent tools run on their own at session start (hooks, MCP servers).
# Executable code in prompts/ (promptctl.py route runs at the start of every
# session). The Markdown instructions beside it are not owned: since
# 2026-11-24 they go through the cyber and legal risk review instead
# (docs/security/risk-review.md).
/prompts/*.py                     @Duncan-Haywood
/prompts/**/*.py                  @Duncan-Haywood
/.claude/                         @Duncan-Haywood
/.cursor/                         @Duncan-Haywood
/.mcp.json                        @Duncan-Haywood
/platform/agents/                 @Duncan-Haywood

